<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">IJssel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The IJssel is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> that leaves it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnhem">Arnhem</a> and flows about 125 kilometres north past Zutphen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampen">Kampen</a> into the IJsselmeer. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a major trade route, and the Hanseatic towns along its banks became some of the richest in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ijssel.jpg" alt="IJssel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tjeerd.nijeholt at Dutch Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deventer_zonsondergang_k.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
